Subject: [PATCH] ipq50xx: bring the radios up when running from the RAM initramfs

The RAM initramfs is the only sanctioned way to write this board's NAND. The
flow in `docs/technical.md` reaches it without serial, but the normal image
comes up with both radios disabled. That is the right default for the installed
system: its generated wireless config initially has no encryption, so enabling
it would bridge an open SSID into br-lan before anyone had set a root password.
The release process applies this change to the separate beaconing initramfs
artifact used for Wi-Fi installation.

Gate it on where the rootfs actually is. `rootfs_type` returns tmpfs exactly
when running from RAM -- the same test `platform_check_image` already uses to
decide whether a sysupgrade is allowed -- so the beaconing artifact stays
silent if it is ever booted as an installed system.

The window this opens is the flash itself: a few minutes, on a system whose
root account has no password either way, with the operator standing next to
it. It closes on the reboot into NAND.

--- a/files/target/linux/qualcommax/ipq50xx/base-files/etc/rc.local
+++ b/files/target/linux/qualcommax/ipq50xx/base-files/etc/rc.local
@@ -17,4 +17,34 @@
 	fw_setenv flag_try_sys2_failed 8 2>/dev/null
 fi

+# Running from RAM => this is the initramfs someone is flashing from, not an
+# installed system. Bring the radios up on a known SSID so the install can be
+# driven over the air; an installed system falls straight through this.
+. /lib/upgrade/common.sh 2>/dev/null
+if [ "$(rootfs_type)" = "tmpfs" ]; then
+	# The wireless config is generated by the mac80211 hotplug at boot; on a
+	# cold first boot rc.local can still win the race, so make sure it exists.
+	[ -s /etc/config/wireless ] || /sbin/wifi config
+
+	INSTALLER_SSID="OpenWrt-RD03v2-Installer"
+	INSTALLER_KEY="rd03v2install"
+
+	for r in 0 1; do
+		uci -q get wireless.radio$r >/dev/null || continue
+		uci -q set wireless.radio$r.disabled='0'
+		uci -q set wireless.default_radio$r.ssid="$INSTALLER_SSID"
+		uci -q set wireless.default_radio$r.encryption='psk2'
+		uci -q set wireless.default_radio$r.key="$INSTALLER_KEY"
+		uci -q set wireless.default_radio$r.disabled='0'
+		uci -q set wireless.default_radio$r.network='lan'
+	done
+	uci -q commit wireless
+	/sbin/wifi reload
+
+	cat >/dev/console <<-EOF
+
+	  RAM initramfs: joinable at $INSTALLER_SSID (WPA2, key $INSTALLER_KEY)
+	  then ssh root@192.168.1.1 and sysupgrade -n the squashfs image.
+
+	EOF
+fi
+
 exit 0

--
Notes for whoever applies this:

* The SSID and key are fixed and public, which is the honest tradeoff for a
  prebuilt image: the alternative is deriving the PSK from the device's own
  MAC, and since the MAC *is* the BSSID that buys nothing against anyone who
  is actually looking. If you would rather not have a known key in a published
  image at all, `encryption='none'` is simpler and no weaker in practice --
  root is passwordless in this window either way.

* Both radios get the same SSID deliberately: the installer does not care
  which band it lands on, and one name is one thing for a user to look for.

* Selecting this artifact does not bypass the NAND gate. The installer still
  verifies the running unit's NAND against the selected release before any
  permanent write.
